<!-- Exercise 067 - MarkupExtensionBasics (intermediate).
     Goal:   Write your own markup extension and use it twice in one view. An
     extension runs at PARSE time and returns the value the property gets, which
     is a different kind of indirection from a binding or a converter.
     Drills: a custom markup extension, ProvideValue, extension properties as
     named arguments in curly-brace syntax.
     Passes: dotnet test, filtered to Ex067_ (see
     Ex067_MarkupExtensionBasics.axaml.cs for the exact command, because an XML
     comment may not hold two hyphens in a row).

     A markup extension in Avalonia needs NO base class and NO interface. Any
     public class with a public ProvideValue method qualifies; XamlX finds it by
     shape. Measured, so do not go looking for MarkupExtension to inherit from. -->
<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:i="clr-namespace:FeWoLearning.Avalonia.Exercises.Intermediate"
             x:Class="FeWoLearning.Avalonia.Exercises.Intermediate.Ex067_MarkupExtensionBasics">
  <!-- TODO: two halves, graded separately.

       First implement ProvideValue in Ex067_Abbreviation (code-behind): it looks
       its Key up in the given Ex067_Glossary.

       Then use it twice here. Replace the placeholder with:

         <StackPanel>
           <TextBlock Name="Mvvm" Text="{i:Ex067_Abbreviation Key=mvvm}" />
           <TextBlock Name="Oaph" Text="{i:Ex067_Abbreviation Key=oaph}" />
         </StackPanel>

       Why two usages with different arguments: one alone could be matched by a
       literal. And because the extension reads the glossary at parse time, the
       test can rewrite the glossary and build a SECOND view - whose text then
       differs from the first's. No literal can follow that, which is what makes
       this exercise gradeable at all. -->
  <StackPanel>
    <TextBlock Text="TODO" />
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
